//! WHAT THE GUARDS MUST SEE. Avra's `string` carries its length; C's
//! ends at the first NUL. So a value crossing into C is judged by the
//! callee on DIFFERENT BYTES than the caller passed.
//!
//! THE SPLIT THIS HEADER USED TO NAME IS GONE. It listed `==`,
//! `contains`, `index_of`, `split` and `replace` as NUL-LOSSY C string
//! calls, against a NUL-SAFE half that read the header. `f57372a`
//! changed all five to `memcmp`/`memmem` over the length. Measured:
//! `"ab\0cd"` has length 5, is UNEQUAL to `"ab"`, `contains("cd")`
//! answers true, `index_of("cd")` answers 3, and splitting on the NUL
//! gives two parts. **A NUL IS AN ORDINARY CHARACTER ON THE AVRA SIDE.**
//!
//! THE RULE IS UNCHANGED AND THE GUARD IS STILL NECESSARY, because the
//! truncation was never on our side of the seam — it is at the EXTERN
//! SEAT and only there. `sqlite3_open` stops at the first NUL whatever
//! Avra can see, so a guard and the thing it guards must still read the
//! same bytes, and a value whose meaning changes at the boundary is not
//! a value anyone meant to pass. What changed is the REASON: nothing
//! here was ever blind, and the door is a boundary door rather than a
//! compensation for lossy primitives.
//!
//! A NUL HAS NO LITERAL SPELLING. `"\0x"` is THREE characters —
//! backslash, `0`, `x` — so `@std/text`'s `from_codepoint` and
//! `from_codepoints` are the only way to mint one.
//!
//! This was not theoretical. A path `from_codepoints([0, 120])` — a NUL
//! then an `x`, length 2 — reaches SQLite as the EMPTY STRING: the
//! private temporary database deleted at close, every write succeeding
//! and the data silently gone. That is the trap this door exists to
//! prevent and it is unaffected by any of the above.






// ── The pointer sentinels ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two of SQLite's arguments are POINTER VALUES rather than pointers to
// anything: the destructor seat of every `bind_*` and `result_*` takes
// either `SQLITE_STATIC` — `(void*)0` — or `SQLITE_TRANSIENT` —
// `(void*)-1` — and SQLite COMPARES the value before it would ever call
// through it (`vendor/sqlite3.h:6429-6430`).

/// `SQLITE_TRANSIENT` itself, from the header, through this package's
/// own C (`src/c/sqlite_sentinel.c`). A DOOR PER CONSTANT RATHER THAN A VERB
/// THAT BUILDS ANY POINTER: a general `ptr_at(address)` would answer
/// this sentinel and every other address besides, and a capability that
/// wide cannot be made safe by a check — only unnecessary.
extern fn avra_sqlite_transient() -> ptr?

/// `SQLITE_TRANSIENT`: SQLite COPIES the bytes before the bind returns,
/// so nothing the caller holds has to outlive anything. This is the
/// default every bind should take.
export fn transient() -> ptr? { avra_sqlite_transient() }

/// `SQLITE_STATIC`: the caller promises the bytes stay valid until the
/// statement is finalized or the parameter is rebound
/// (`vendor/sqlite3.h:4946-4950`). Avra cannot enforce that promise —
/// a released box is a use-after-free INSIDE SQLite, where the
/// runtime's refcounter is blind by construction — so this is a named
/// hatch and never a default.
export fn static_bytes() -> ptr? { null }

// ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE A REAL CALLBACK IS EVER BOUND.
// `SQLITE_TRANSIENT` is a FUNCTION pointer type, and clang SIGNS
// function pointers on arm64e — it does not sign pointers to ordinary
// data. `(void*)-1` is safe here only because SQLite compares it and
// never calls through it. The day this driver binds a real destructor,
// or any other C callback, that stops being true and the pointer has to
// be signed like the function pointer it is.
